/// `Fallible` — the protocol `?` resolves through for a type that is not
/// `Option` or `Result` (RFC-0080 M3).
///
/// Propagation in Vyrn is a copy of the whole sum, not a destructure-and-rebuild:
/// `x?` returns the value it was given, byte for byte, into a function of the
/// same type. So the protocol owes exactly two answers and no more — which side
/// of the sum a value is on, and how to read the success payload. There is no
/// residual to name (Rust's `FromResidual` exists because its `?` changes types)
/// and no reconstruction step.
///
/// A consequence: variant count is free. An enum with two failing variants
/// propagates whichever one it holds, payload intact, because nothing takes it
/// apart.
///
///     impl Fallible for Http {
///         type Output = Body
///         fn isSuccess(self) -> Bool { .. }
///         fn success(self) -> Body { .. }   // total: `panic` on the failing arms
///     }
///
/// `success` is called only after `isSuccess` answered true, so its failing arms
/// are unreachable; they still have to return something, and `panic` (RFC-0079
/// M1) is what says so.

export protocol Fallible {
    type Output
    fn isSuccess(self) -> Bool
    fn success(self) -> Output
}
